## Results

| Run | Scenario | Exact nonce marker | Retired / stop | Delivered #PF | UART bytes / SHA-256 |
|---|---|---|---|---:|---|
| 0001 | passed | valid | 2,599,999,998 / `budget_exhausted` | 34 | 24,074 / `3382510911f37fe024fe601c9bfd5925e34d9ee872a15827e4efa463e14533fe` |
| 0002 | passed | valid | 2,599,999,998 / `budget_exhausted` | 33 | 24,131 / `f05bd2209b3277597ae76d960292ad8c66a534a11cdba9f987ea8a6fa62ffb9c` |

In both UART captures, `[    2.271...] Run /init as init process` appears on its
own line before the exact `OD_NONCE=<run nonce>` line. Both nonce values were
unique and validated exactly. This reproduces the desired marker order in two
consecutive runs with the candidate guest fixture.

The strict comparator still returned `matches=false`. Its unexplained
differences were `run[1].machine_observations` and
`run[1].ordered_uart_bytes` after existing nonce normalization. The delivered
page-fault counts also differ (34 versus 33); the UART captures differ in
length and hash. No new normalization was introduced, and no remaining
differences were declared benign. Thus this
experiment supports the timing hypothesis for the marker interleaving only;
it does not make S5-07 acceptable and does not explain the remaining machine or
UART variation.

